findings.description:
Scope L70-71 promises a `density` field, and plan L26 requires workloads to specify "N, density, profile mix, formation state". The schema has no density field. Density can only be inferred from `n`/`cluster_radius`/`world_size`, and the design never defines it.

The schema also doesn't specify:
- how each formation kind samples positions (uniform disk vs. Gaussian, for example);
- initial velocities or headings (`AgentState.create` gives zeros);
- how fractions turn into counts (rounding, ordering).

Only `mid-flock` is shown. The parameters for `dispersed` and `packed-herd` (N, radius) aren't given, so "high/low density" can't be checked. And parity-hash determinism across implementations depends on exactly these details.

`tick.py` has work between the named phases:
- profile-attribute arrays and per-agent gathers (L58-72), after build and before query;
- `exclude_self` and `apply_neighbor_cap`, mixed into both query branches (L81-102). The architecture counts these as "CSR assembly" (`self_excl_cap`, arch L54);
- the `max_speeds` gather (L114);
- `record_density` (L129-130).

The design never says which phase each of these goes in. The Risk section (L697-701) says `force_total` includes `all_radii = np.array(...)`, but that runs before the spatial query, not in the force loop.

`total_ns` is defined two ways. The design says "tick wall time, including overhead" (L284). The existing code says "spatial_build through end of integrate" (`runner.py:27-30`), which leaves out `record_density`. The duplication is also understated as "~5 lines per phase" (L266-267). `tick_instrumented` has to copy the whole ~90-line body, both query branches included.

None of these is handled:
- `git rev-parse HEAD` failing (no git, or not in a repo, e.g. a copy on an NVIDIA box);
- a dirty working tree, which makes "reproducible by repo hash" (L184-185) false unless it's recorded. The example's short SHA `fc989bf` (L418) also doesn't match `rev-parse HEAD`'s full SHA;
- two runs in the same second overwriting `{timestamp}_{workload}_{backend}.json`;
- ISO timestamps with `:` in filenames, which are invalid on Windows. That matters for a "cross-platform" harness;
- `bench/results/` resolved against the current directory, not the repo root;
- a run killed mid-write, leaving a truncated JSON that the directory-wide Markdown pass (L440) then hits. Write to a temp file and rename;
- exceptions from `tick_once` mid-measure. Only `setup` failures are listed.

With one registered backend, cut or defer:
- `--all-backends` (L481), which has nothing to run beyond `--backend numpy`;
- `--workload-dir` (L482);
- the cross-run Markdown pass over `bench/results/` (L440). Render the current run only;
- reusing `pytest-benchmark` in the stub-backend tests (L122-124). Timing-accounting tests on synthetic timings don't need it.

The Value section (L36-51) mostly repeats the plan entry. "Eight new files" (L741) understates the 11 Python modules and 3 YAML files in Component Structure (L157-181).
